## Data gaps
- The exact 5%-vs-100% traffic exposure arithmetic is not directly measured; the claim that the flag ramp caused the CPU step is supported by timing, naming and the flat offered load, but the per-path compute-share is not instrumented.
- Whether the GreeksV2 code path itself was introduced before 2026-05-15 is outside deploy-history reach (history starts 2026-05-15); both a flag-value regression and a pre-window code change converge on the same GreeksV2 origin, but the two are not separated.
- redis-cache telemetry (cache hit/miss, eviction, latency) was never retrieved, so an unmeasured cache-side event coincident with the 12:04-12:05Z onset cannot be formally excluded, although the CPU-bound signature with flat memory and no restart argues against it.
- No pricing-engine metric exposes thread-pool, queue-depth or GC-time series, so a downstream lock/queue wait cannot be directly distinguished from pure CPU-bound compute; the CPU-bound conclusion rests on cpu_pct=100% plus p99~2.6s.
- Metrics are no finer than 60s (240s default), so a sub-minute lag between the 12:04:00Z config write and the 12:05 CPU bucket cannot be resolved; onset ordering rests on log timestamps at minute resolution.
- order-service and pricing-engine log results were truncated (40 of 227 and 40 of 619 matched), so additional distinct error signatures after 12:25Z cannot be excluded.
- The config-audit shard 2/3 was initially unresponsive; although the completed rescan (F27) and per-service rescans (F28) closed this for the 10:00-13:59Z window, absence of changes on other services outside those rescans is not proven.
- No component named 'quote-service' exists in the catalog, so the literal 'quote-service config change' branch of the task is unanswerable; the quote path was instead covered via order-service -> pricing-engine.

## What would change this conclusion
- pricing-engine cpu_pct, p99 and error_rate do NOT return to pre-12:05 baselines after rolling back cfg-3120 to 5%, which would falsify H1 and re-open the CPU-saturation/load hypothesis (H4).
- A service-side metric showing pricing-engine request_rate or compute work rose independently of the flag (e.g. a request-rate step at 12:04-12:05Z) would support organic load growth (H4) instead of H1 - but request_rate is currently flat (F21).
- Discovery of another quote-path change (e.g. a config/flag change on a previously unscanned shard, or a pricing-engine deploy near 12:04Z) that also explains the GreeksV2 CPU saturation would deprioritize cfg-3120; the completed rescans found only cfg-3118 and cfg-3120.
- Retrieving redis-cache telemetry showing a cache stall/eviction event at 12:04-12:05Z would revive the shared-cache hypothesis (H3), though the current CPU-bound, flat-memory, no-restart signature and the GreeksV2 Monte-Carlo WARN text argue against it.
- Evidence that market-data-service v5.2.0 directly increased pricing-engine's compute cost (e.g. a pricing-engine log line showing protobuf quote parse/expand taking seconds) would challenge the topology argument - but market-data-service latency is flat and the slow log line is named GreeksV2Calculator, not a serializer.
- Demonstration that the GreeksV2 compute cost is NOT volume/path-share dependent (e.g. the calculator running equally slow at 5% exposure) would undercut the assumption that the ramp, not something else, drove the CPU step.

